">How Network Redesign Cut Transportation Costs by 22% in 90 Days

Stop Chasing Cheaper Freight Rates. Redesign the Network Instead.

A national home goods retailer came to us frustrated and exhausted.

They renegotiated carrier rates every year.
They rebid freight lanes.
They consolidated volume.
They benchmarked competitors.
They pushed back on accessorial.
They tried every lever on the carrier side of the equation.
Yet transportation costs kept rising.
Customer delivery times were slipping.
Carrier performance was inconsistent.
Teams were firefighting instead of improving.
Everyone believed the problem was pricing.
But the real issue wasn=E2=80=99t the carriers.
It was the network design.

The company=E2=80=99s distribution footprint was built for a business that =
no longer existed:
 =E2=80=A2 E-commerce volume was a fraction of today
 =E2=80=A2 Store locations had shifted over time
 =E2=80=A2 Customer demand patterns moved closer to the coasts
 =E2=80=A2 Product mix changed (more parcel, fewer pallets)

The network was optimized for yesterday, not today.
So the business was:
 =E2=80=A2 Shipping orders too far
 =E2=80=A2 Running low-density lanes
 =E2=80=A2 Paying long-haul rates for short-haul demand
 =E2=80=A2 Using national carriers where regional carriers were more cost-e=
ffective
 =E2=80=A2 Routing replenishment through the wrong nodes
No amount of rate negotiation can offset structural inefficiency.

What we changed:
Mapped actual customer demand flows by ZIP, channel mix, and SKU velocity
Identified lane density breakdowns where order volume couldn=E2=80=99t supp=
ort efficient routing
Re-modeled distribution zones to minimize last-mile distance and reduce tou=
ches
Introduced regional + hybrid carriers to replace national coverage where it=
 wasn=E2=80=99t needed
Re-sequenced fulfillment logic to default shipments to the closest viable n=
ode every time
90-Day Results:
=E2=9C=94 Transportation cost per order down 22%
 =E2=9C=94 On-time delivery improved from 87% =E2=86=92 96%
 =E2=9C=94 Customer escalations dropped significantly
 =E2=9C=94 Carrier dependency risk decreased
 =E2=9C=94 No new buildings, no new systems =E2=80=94 just better network l=
ogic

The insight:
Freight cost isn=E2=80=99t determined in the rate sheet.
It=E2=80=99s determined in how the network is designed.
You don=E2=80=99t solve freight by pushing carriers harder.
You solve freight by reducing miles, touches, and unnecessary complexity.
Network design is the lever that moves cost, service, and growth =E2=80=94 =
simultaneously.
I can support with =E2=80=9CNetwork Cost Reset=E2=80=9D for retailers, DTC =
brands, and omni-channel operators looking to reduce freight cost without s=
acrificing service.

