owth hides inefficiency
Until margin forces it into the open





At $100M, revenue can still cover inefficiency.
For a while.

But eventually, margin starts to tell the truth.

What shows up:
=E2=80=A2 Cost to serve increasing faster than revenue
=E2=80=A2 Duplication across teams and functions
=E2=80=A2 Inconsistent processes across locations
=E2=80=A2 Rising rework and inefficiencies

In many businesses:
=E2=80=A2 Operating costs grow 3 to 6 points faster than revenue
=E2=80=A2 Margin compression starts quietly

This is often when I get the call.
Not because revenue stopped.
Because profitability stopped improving.

A true operational review typically reveals:
=E2=80=A2 Where cost is being absorbed
=E2=80=A2 Where process breakdowns exist
=E2=80=A2 Where standardization is needed

And once addressed:
EBITDA improves without needing drastic action.

Revenue creates momentum.
Margin forces discipline.

Question for founders:
If revenue slowed tomorrow, where would inefficiency show up first?<button =
