0M companies are not underperforming
They are outgrowing their operating model...




I have spent time inside several companies in the $75M to $150M range.
From the outside, they look strong.

Revenue is growing.
Demand is there.
Teams are busy.

But internally, a different pattern shows up.

The operating model that got them to $100M no longer supports the next stag=
e.

What I typically see:
=E2=80=A2 Decisions still centralized around the founder
=E2=80=A2 Functional silos starting to form
=E2=80=A2 Planning happening reactively instead of proactively
=E2=80=A2 Execution speed slowing despite team growth

This is usually where I get pulled in.

Not because the business is broken.
Because it is starting to feel heavier than it should.

At this stage, the work is not about fixing people.
It is about realigning the operating model to match the scale of the busine=
ss.

Most companies do not stall because they lack opportunity.
They stall because structure has not caught up to growth.

Question for CEOs
