d=3D"expandable-text-box">Silent EBITDA Killer #4
Network Design Drift


One of the most overlooked EBITDA drivers in retail isn=E2=80=99t cost.
It=E2=80=99s network design.

And more specifically=E2=80=A6
How far it has drifted from reality.

Most retail supply chains weren=E2=80=99t designed.

They evolved.
=E2=80=A2 New DCs added to solve short-term problems
=E2=80=A2 Delivery zones built around geography instead of density
=E2=80=A2 Transportation lanes layered over time

At some point, the network stops reflecting the business.
And starts reflecting its history.

That=E2=80=99s when costs begin to rise quietly.

What I=E2=80=99ve seen repeatedly:
=E2=80=A2 Freight costs climbing without clear explanation
=E2=80=A2 Delivery inefficiencies increasing
=E2=80=A2 Inventory positioned in the wrong places
=E2=80=A2 Longer lead times creeping in

Then when the network is actually redesigned around current demand patterns=
 and volume flows, the shift is immediate:
=E2=80=A2 Freight cost improves
=E2=80=A2 Delivery efficiency increases
=E2=80=A2 Inventory becomes more productive
=E2=80=A2 Service levels improve

And EBITDA follows.

Not because of cuts.
Because the system was realigned.

Growth changes the business.
But many companies never redesign the system to match it.

Question for founders and CEOs:

Does your current network reflect your strategy=E2=80=A6 or your history?<b=
